// A CALLEE HANDED A WAY TO THE ASSIGNMENT TARGET'S CONTAINER, INSIDE THE WINDOW (DEF-415, landing 177a). `List<int64>->:q = @l; l[0i64] = raw grow(q);` addressed `l[0]`, the
// callee reallocated `l` through the alias, and the store landed in FREED memory (`p6_alias_store`, 67 on 175 and 176b: the store lost): DEF-410 (d)'s target party is keyed by `l`, and the
// growth was a READ of `q`. Now a held alias, a copy of one, a struct holding one, `@l`, the implicit `@recv` and a root pointer, handed to ANY callee inside an assignment's value, is judged WHOLE
// against the live targets alone (`target_handed`), the callee's summary UNREAD, because a summary misses writes (`q2_helper`, DEF-298's writer face; `q2_change`, DEF-357), and BY PATH, so
// `e.binds[0] = raw grow(@e.syms)` stays legal (`assign_target_handed_ok.npk`). What the value holds is read off its refs, one hop per holder, a holder of a holder too; past the walk's fuel of 8 it refuses
// (`dt_chain10`, NITPICK-BORROW-007). A callee that writes nothing (`d415_readonly`, `r_e_read`, `v_e_addr_read`) or only a SIBLING of the target's container (`r_e_other`, `r_e_copy_other`, `v_e_addr_other`, the shape of the pointer root's nine `src/` sites), and an `o` that holds a pointer beside it (`r2_holder`, `o.binds[0] = raw rd(o)`), are over-refused, the post-180 relaxation with `l[i] = raw f(@l)`; the road DEF-418 names is the emitter's order.
mod:assign_target_handed;
error:E5;
func:grow = int64(List<int64>->:p) never fails { for (int64:i in 0i64...200i64) { drop list_push(p, i); } pass 9i64; };
func:mk_list = List<int64>() never fails {
    List<int64>:l = raw list_init::<int64>(2i64);
    drop list_push(@l, 7i64);
    drop list_push(@l, 8i64);
    pass l;
};
